{"id":71,"course_id":21,"title":"Pipeline Security and Secrets","summary":"Protect deploy permissions and credentials in automation.","sequence":4,"body_simplified":"## Mental model\nCI/CD systems are production control planes. Runner isolation, secret scoping, OIDC federation, approvals, and audit logs prevent pipelines from becoming a backdoor.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice reviewing repo permissions, environment protection, deploy roles, and secret exposure paths.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nThreat-model a pipeline that can deploy to prod and list the minimum guardrails before launch.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","body_traditional":"## Mental model\nCI/CD systems are production control planes. Runner isolation, secret scoping, OIDC federation, approvals, and audit logs prevent pipelines from becoming a backdoor.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice reviewing repo permissions, environment protection, deploy roles, and secret exposure paths.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nThreat-model a pipeline that can deploy to prod and list the minimum guardrails before launch.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","pinyin":"Scenario: You are the platform engineer on call for a realistic team environment.\nChecklist:\n- State the user impact in one sentence.\n- Identify the system boundary and owner.\n- Collect read-only evidence first.\n- Propose the smallest reversible change.\n- Capture what should become a dashboard, alert, runbook, or automation.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\n$ kubectl describe pod POD_NAME -n NAMESPACE\n$ kubectl logs deploy/APP -n NAMESPACE --since=15m","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-cicd-release-engineering","course_category":"CI/CD","course_era":"Platform Academy","vocabulary":[{"id":326,"simplified":"CI/CD and Release Engineering","traditional":"CI/CD and Release Engineering","pinyin":"CI/CD","definition":"Build safe pipelines for container builds, tests, image signing, environment promotion, progressive delivery, and rollback."},{"id":327,"simplified":"Evidence","traditional":"Evidence","pinyin":"Debugging","definition":"Observed facts from commands, dashboards, logs, traces, events, or cloud APIs."},{"id":328,"simplified":"Blast radius","traditional":"Blast radius","pinyin":"Reliability","definition":"The set of users, tenants, workloads, or systems affected by a failure or change."},{"id":329,"simplified":"Rollback","traditional":"Rollback","pinyin":"Operations","definition":"A planned path to return to a known-good state when a change causes harm."},{"id":330,"simplified":"Runbook","traditional":"Runbook","pinyin":"Operations","definition":"A step-by-step guide that helps responders diagnose and mitigate an incident safely."}],"flashcards":[{"id":196,"lesson_id":71,"prompt":"What is the main goal of Pipeline Security and Secrets?","answer":"Protect deploy permissions and credentials in automation.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":197,"lesson_id":71,"prompt":"Why collect evidence before changing live systems?","answer":"It reduces guessing, protects users, and makes the fix reviewable.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":198,"lesson_id":71,"prompt":"What should a learner produce after each lab?","answer":"A clear diagnosis, a safe action plan, and a reusable note for future incidents.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
